The Archive must contain the following files:

BirthDays/
          BirthdayDoor_V3.0     (The Door ItSelf)
          BirthDay.text         (Ansi To Display On BirthDay)
          Foo Bar            (Example User Date Text File) 
          BirthdayDoor_V3.0.Doc (Your Reading It)

---------------------------------------------------------------------
                          What it Does:
---------------------------------------------------------------------
	It checks for a data file in the Doors:BirthDays/
directory and if there is no data file for the user it then
asks them for their birthdate and saves it into said directory,
if the data file is there it look at it and if there is no
match in the dates it exits but if there is a match it then
displays an ANSI screen and adds 30 minutes to there time 
as a gift from the SysOp, if you don't like this addition
don't use this door, but I think that it's fair as we all
only get one bithday a year and this is a cost free gift
to the users that just happen to login on their birthday
and that is rare.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
                          Installation:
---------------------------------------------------------------------
	Easy to do just copy the whole BirthDays directory across
to your doors directory then add it to your MainDoor.text.

Eg. BirthDays/BirthDayDoor_V1.9

You can also edit the ansi to look how you want, but don't forget
the screen clearing code at the top and the %Z at the bottom.
And thats about it.

All users that logon once it has been installed
will be asked once for their date of birth and it will add their
file to the BirthDays directory and check it each time they logon,
it is also a good idea to check these files from time to time to 
make sure that the users have filled the date out right the date
format is: MM-DD-YYYY :if it is not right just edit it in a text
editor.
The data files are very small (11 Bytes each) I have 107 so far
and the total room they takeup is 1177 Bytes or 1.177 K.

